  "narrative": "All reported estimates are already signed to the registered hypothesis direction, so a positive estimate supports the claimed direction and a negative estimate opposes it. The feature_003 subgroup mean differences are directionally consistent with the accepted overall feature_003 association but do not, by themselves, justify accepting subgroup-specific threshold claims. H13, H14, H15, and H21 are rejected: H14 and H21 have point estimates below the 0.15-unit threshold, and H13/H15 are only marginally above threshold while their intervals include substantial subthreshold uncertainty. This is especially important because these are exploratory subgroup probes after an already accepted main effect; accepting each borderline subgroup would inflate multiplicity. H16 and H17 are the only subgroup direct effects with point estimates clearly above threshold and intervals excluding zero, but their lower bounds are still below threshold and cell sizes are smaller. Given that feature_038>=0.5 and age>=60 are not biologically established effect modifiers and the corresponding interactions are weak or null, I leave H16/H17 unresolved but deferred rather than validating them now. The interaction claims H18-H20 test heterogeneity of the feature_003 association. H18 and H19 have point estimates near threshold but intervals include zero and are too uncertain to support effect modification; H20 is clearly below threshold. I therefore reject the feature_003 interaction claims: the current evidence is more consistent with an overall dataset-level feature_003 association than with strong, validated heterogeneity. The new binary predictor scans H22 and H23 are small and compatible with null. H24 is different: the signed estimate is -0.810 under direction -1, which opposes the registered stronger-dependency direction and implies a very large raw positive exposed-minus-comparator contrast, i.e. feature_002=1 is associated with much higher outcome_001 values/weaker composite